text:
zara: quick a11y review of the new "keyboard shortcuts" overlay. Found issues: trap not reliable, screen readers read the overlay twice, and high-contrast theme breaks the icon colors.

kevin: thanks. which browser/AT and how to repro?

zara: repro (macOS+VoiceOver, Chrome+NVDA): open with ? or click Help > Shortcuts, tab into overlay, try to close with ESC. Sometimes focus escapes to the page behind; VO announces the content on open and then again when focus lands on first control. Also the shortcut glyphs use CSS masks and lose contrast in system high-contrast mode.

a11y-bot: Automated checks: role="dialog" present but aria-modal missing (WARNING). Focus trap test: fails intermittently in simulated tab sequence (FAIL). Icon contrast: computed fg #b0b7c3 on bg #ffffff => 2.6:1 (FAIL).

nina: we render overlay as a non-modal region for keyboard users so devs could interact with background. Should be a true modal for keyboard-only workflows.

ben: agree. overlay should: 1) set role="dialog" + aria-modal="true" 2) trap focus inside while open 3) return focus to opener on close 4) close on ESC and click-outside 5) update live region only once on open.

lucy: small detail: VoiceOver will sometimes re-announce if we set focus to the first control immediately after rendering. Workaround: render dialog with inert/aria-hidden on the background, then set focus to dialog container (or first focusable) after a short t=0 tick, not mid-render.

zara: here's suggested markup + focus-trap pseudocode:

```html
<!-- markup -->
<div id="shortcut-dialog" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="shortcuts-title" class="dialog" hidden>
  <h2 id="shortcuts-title">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
  <button class="close" aria-label="Close shortcuts">✕</button>
  <ul>
    <li><kbd>?</kbd> Open shortcuts</li>
    <li><kbd>g then d</kbd> Go to dashboard</li>
  </ul>
</div>
```

```js
// focus trap pseudocode
const opener = document.activeElement;
openDialog = () => {
  // make background inert
  document.body.querySelectorAll('#app > *:not(#shortcut-dialog)').forEach(el => el.setAttribute('aria-hidden','true'));
  dialog.hidden = false;
  requestAnimationFrame(() => dialog.querySelector('.close')?.focus());
}
closeDialog = () => {
  dialog.hidden = true;
  document.body.querySelectorAll('#app > *:not(#shortcut-dialog)').forEach(el => el.removeAttribute('aria-hidden'));
  if (opener && document.contains(opener)) opener.focus();
}
// trap tab/shift+tab inside dialog keydown handler
```

ben: note the use of requestAnimationFrame helps avoid double announce. Also don't mutate innerHTML on open/close as that can trigger extra announcements.

kevin: about high-contrast icons: prefer using SVG with explicit fill colors rather than CSS masks, and provide fallback text (sr-only) for screen reader users. Also in forced high-contrast modes, ensure we have a high-contrast stylesheet: icons to at least 4.5:1 for critical actions.

nina: accessibility testing notes: run axe-core + manual checks on VO/NVDA for open/close announcements, tabbable order test, and a keyboard-only navigation script that tabs 50 times to ensure trap holds. Also screenshot high-contrast mode and run contrast analysis for glyphs.

a11y-bot: Queued pre-merge checks: aria-modal, focus-trap simulation, icon contrast, sr-only presence.

lucy: small UX nit: when overlay is open, also visually dim background but keep contrast preserved for dialog. Use backdrop with rgba(0,0,0,0.4) and ensure dialog background meets contrast with text.

zara: will open a PR with markup/CSS/JS + test plan. PR summary: "Make keyboard shortcuts overlay a true modal: aria-modal, reliable focus trap, focus restore, prevent duplicate SR announcements, and fix icon contrast for high-contrast mode." tagging @kevin for review. :raised_hands:
